= Dataset <chapter:dataset>

The data used in this study are based on hydraulic base scenarios for the Hanoi water distribution network and arsenic contamination events. These components form the common basis for the precomputed offline dataset and the interaction data generated during online policy training.

For the offline dataset, additional predefined chlorine injection profiles are generated. Each profile is combined with a hydraulic base scenario and an arsenic schedule to compute and store the complete network response in advance as a trajectory for pretraining. During online policy training, chlorine inputs are instead generated by the policy as it interacts with the simulator.

@fig:dataset-at-a-glance summarizes how the shared hydraulic scenarios, arsenic schedules, and chlorine inputs form the precomputed offline trajectories and online interaction data.

#figure(
  include "dataset-at-a-glance.typ",
  kind: image,
  caption: [Construction of offline trajectories and online interaction data from shared hydraulic base scenarios. Each cell in the offline matrix represents one pairing of a chlorine profile and an arsenic schedule. Online policy training uses only the training split.],
) <fig:dataset-at-a-glance>

Before any derived data are generated, the hydraulic base scenarios are assigned to separate training, validation, and test partitions. Every precomputed offline trajectory, online policy-training episode, and fixed control case inherits the partition of its base scenario. The exact allocation is reported in @section:dataset:splits.

The chapter first introduces the hydraulic base scenarios and arsenic contamination events. It then describes the simulation framework, offline trajectories, and online interaction data, followed by the dataset partitions and fixed control cases, before concluding with reproducibility details and a dataset summary.


== Hydraulic Base Scenarios <section:dataset:hydraulic-scenarios>

The study uses 100 hydraulic base scenarios, labeled `scenario-0` to `scenario-99`. Each scenario combines the Hanoi network model with generated 25-day demand patterns for each junction and a fixed set of sampled network parameters. The network model, demand patterns, and parameter uncertainties are described in the following subsections. Together, they define the hydraulic conditions of each scenario, which remain fixed whenever it is reused.

The collection is limited to 100 scenarios to keep trajectory generation, model training, and repeated validation within the computational budget while maintaining separate scenario-level training, validation, and test partitions.


=== Hanoi Water Distribution Network <section:dataset:hanoi-network>

The Hanoi water distribution network was introduced by #cite-prose(<fujiwara1990twophase>) as a mathematical model based on a planned trunk distribution system for Hanoi, Vietnam. It served as a test case for the cost-optimal design of a looped water distribution network. In this study, it provides the physical basis for the hydraulic and water-quality simulations used to generate the dataset.

The network comprises one fixed-head reservoir, 31 demand junctions, and 34 pipes arranged in three loops, with two terminal junctions. The reservoir is denoted as node 1 and supplies the network at a fixed hydraulic head of #meter[100]. Junctions 2--32 represent the locations where water is withdrawn to meet consumer demand. The topology and node numbering are shown in @fig:hanoi-topology.

#figure(
  include "hanoi-topology.typ",
  kind: image,
  caption: [Topology and node numbering of the Hanoi water distribution network used in this study. The diamond denotes reservoir node 1, circles denote the 31 demand junctions, and lines denote the 34 pipes. The layout is schematic, and line lengths do not represent physical pipe lengths.],
) <fig:hanoi-topology>

In the classical Hanoi network optimization problem, pipe diameters are treated as design variables. In this study, all scenarios share the topology and pipe diameter configuration of the model provided through EPyT-Flow @artelt2026epytflow017. Hydraulic variation instead arises from differences in nodal demand patterns, base demand, pipe length, and pipe roughness.

This study uses reservoir node 1 as the only chlorine-injection location. Chlorine is introduced there as a mass-rate source and transported through the pipes with the hydraulic flow to the demand junctions.


=== Demand Patterns

Nodal demand patterns describe how water demand varies over time. For each hydraulic base scenario, we generate a distinct set of these patterns using the LeakDB demand-pattern generator integrated into EPyT-Flow @artelt2026epytflow017. LeakDB was developed as a benchmark dataset for leakage diagnosis in water distribution networks @vrachimis2018leakdb. Its demand patterns combine weekly and seasonal variation with random fluctuations. Scenario-specific random seeds make the patterns reproducible. Their assignment is documented in @section:appendix:dataset-artifacts.

The generator produces demand patterns covering 365 days. For this study, we use the first 25 consecutive days, beginning at day 0, so all scenarios cover the same calendar period. Restricting the demand period to 25 days limits the computational cost. The selected demand sequence remains fixed whenever a base scenario is reused. @fig:demand-characteristics shows the nodal water demand for one example scenario over this period, with weekly and daily excerpts showing variation at shorter timescales.

#figure(
  demand-figure,
  kind: image,
