=== Network Parameter Uncertainties

In addition to the scenario-specific demand patterns, this study varies base demand, pipe length, and pipe roughness. Each affected value is multiplied by a factor sampled uniformly between #num[0.8] and #num[1.2], permitting deviations of up to #sym.plus.minus$thin$#pct[20]. The demand-pattern multipliers also receive additive Gaussian perturbations with a mean of zero and a standard deviation of #num[0.1].

These perturbations are sampled once for each scenario using a scenario-specific random seed as documented in @section:appendix:dataset-artifacts. The resulting uncertainty realization remains fixed whenever the scenario is reused. Together with the demand patterns, it determines the scenario's hydraulic conditions.


== Arsenic Contamination Events <section:dataset:arsenic-events>

While demand patterns and network parameter uncertainties affect the hydraulic conditions over time and between scenarios, we introduce arsenic events as chemical disturbances to which the chlorine controller must respond.

Each event introduces #acr("As(III)") at one source junction for a predetermined duration with a constant rate. The event is represented as a mass-rate source, meaning that the injected arsenic strength is specified as a mass flow rate in #mg-min() rather than directly as a concentration. The injected arsenite creates a local concentration contribution that varies with nodal outflow, as described in @section:foundations:transport-mixing-mass-rate. In this simulation, reactions with chlorine occur only in downstream pipes. @fig:arsenic-spatial-propagation illustrates downstream transport for a relatively mild and a relatively strong event, followed by temporally overlapping source events.

Multiple arsenic events with varying start time, duration, location, and severity may occur during a single simulation run. All contamination events assigned to one specific run are specified by an arsenic schedule, which includes each event's start time, duration, source junction, and injection rate.

#figure(
  arsenic-figure,
  placement: none,
  kind: image,
  caption: arsenic-caption,
) <fig:arsenic-spatial-propagation>


=== Event Scheduler

A common scheduler defines all arsenic event schedules used throughout the study. The scheduler produces finite sets of fixed schedules for the precomputed offline dataset, generates a new schedule for each use of a scenario during online policy training, and produces separate fixed schedule sets for control validation and testing. The same event-generation rules therefore apply to all three uses. Fixed arsenic schedules are labeled `arsenic-<index>`, where the index starts at zero within each base scenario and schedule set.

Each schedule comprises 1--4 arsenic events, with all event counts having equal probability. Each event introduces arsenic at a randomly selected eligible junction at a constant, randomly sampled mass injection rate. Events at different junctions may overlap in time, but the same junction cannot be affected by two events at the same time.


=== Event Parameter Sampling

Arsenic contamination events are restricted to the middle 21 days of each 25-day simulation run, leaving two injection-free days at both the beginning and the end. Both injection-free periods are part of the evaluation protocol described in @section:evaluation:episodes. Event durations are sampled in 30-minute increments between 2 and #hour[72]. Start times lie on the same 30-minute grid and are distributed across the weekly cycle within the 21-day event window.

Arsenic sources are selected from junctions 2--32, excluding the two terminal junctions 13 and 22, leaving 29 eligible source locations. Reservoir node 1 is also excluded as an injection source. After the duration and weekly start position have been sampled, the scheduler considers all eligible junction--week combinations. Sampling the weekly start position first aims to give each weekday an equal probability of an event start, despite duration constraints at the end of the final week. A combination remains eligible only if the complete event fits within the 21-day window, does not overlap another event at the same junction, and the local outflow permits at least one injection rate that satisfies the concentration and mass-rate limits described below. The junction and week are then sampled from these eligible combinations, and the exact event strength is determined afterward.

The event strength is selected according to the water outflow at the affected junction over the event interval. The resulting immediate local arsenic contribution remains between #num[0.1] and #mg-l[10] throughout the event, while the mass injection rate is limited to #mg-min[50000] per event. These bounds are intended to produce disturbances that affect chlorine concentrations while remaining manageable through adjustments to chlorine injection. Both limits apply to each event separately. Simultaneous events may therefore exceed the mass-rate limit in total, and arsenic transported from another event may add to the concentration observed at an affected node.

@fig:arsenic-event-coverage summarizes the event characteristics and spatial reach of the fixed control-validation cases described in @section:dataset:splits, with all cases simulated without chlorine. For the spatial-reach analysis, transported #acr("As(III)") excludes the estimated immediate local contribution of newly injected arsenic at source nodes. Source events are grouped into contamination clusters when they are not separated by persistent clearance of the transported concentration sum across monitored nodes at #mg-l[0.01]. The complete sampling procedure is documented in @section:appendix:arsenic-schedule-generation.

