== Simulation Framework and Recorded Variables <section:dataset:simulation-setup>

Rather than collecting network responses from a physical water distribution system, this study generates them through simulation. Each simulation run combines one hydraulic base scenario, one arsenic schedule, and one chlorine input sequence. The resulting ordered sequence of hydraulic and water-quality states forms a trajectory.

The hydraulic states are computed using EPANET @rossman2020epanet, while #acr("EPANET-MSX") uses these states to transport two bulk species, free chlorine and #acr("As(III)"), and to model their reactions within the pipes @shang2023epanetmsx. Chlorine undergoes first-order decay and also reacts with #acr("As(III)"), while longitudinal dispersion is not included. Only free chlorine and #acr("As(III)") are tracked, while the oxidation product As(V) is not represented explicitly. Chlorine-induced oxidation of #acr("As(III)") therefore does not represent removal of total arsenic from the water. The pipe reaction rates are given in @eq:appendix-pipe-reaction-rates, and the simulator settings are documented in @section:appendix:simulation-configuration.

Hydraulic states and network outputs are computed and recorded at 30-minute intervals, while #acr("EPANET-MSX") advances water-quality transport and reactions using an internal five-minute time step. Each simulation run covers 25 days, corresponding to 1200 30-minute intervals. The simulation records the initial state at #hour[0] and the state after each interval, giving 1201 samples through the #hour[600] endpoint. At the start of each simulation, chlorine injection and the concentrations of chlorine and #acr("As(III)") throughout the network are initialized to zero.

Chlorine and #acr("As(III)") concentrations are reported in #mg-l(), while source mass injection rates are expressed in #mg-min(). At an injection node, newly introduced #acr("As(III)") is mixed with the outgoing water, but no chlorine--arsenic reaction is modeled at the junction itself. The reaction begins after the water enters a pipe.

The recorded network variables used in this study are demand, chlorine concentration, and #acr("As(III)") concentration at junctions 2--32, together with the flow through all 34 pipes. Node 1 remains the reservoir and chlorine source and is therefore not included among the monitored demand junctions. Recorded values are exact simulator outputs without any artificially added noise.

The stored hydraulic results of each base scenario are reused to determine nodal water outflow from demand and pipe flows. This outflow is used to set the arsenic mass injection rates described in @section:dataset:arsenic-events. Only a subset of the recorded variables is used as model inputs.


== Offline Dataset Construction

The precomputed offline trajectories provide the fixed simulation data used by the pretraining pipeline. Each trajectory combines a fixed arsenic schedule with a predefined chlorine injection profile and stores the resulting hydraulic and water-quality response of the network. The aim is to provide examples from which a forecasting model can learn how chlorine concentrations evolve under different injection sequences and operating conditions. For this purpose, the injection profiles vary in their temporal structure to generate a range of network responses. The following sections describe the chlorine injection profiles, fixed arsenic schedule sets, and resulting trajectories.


=== Chlorine Injection Profiles

Prescribed chlorine injection profiles are generated in advance. For each of the 100 hydraulic base scenarios, five such profiles are generated, resulting in 500 profiles across the complete offline dataset. Each profile contains 1200 actions, one for each executed 30-minute interval. Profiles are labeled `chlorine-<index>`, with indices from 0 to 4 within each base scenario.

Profile generation begins by dividing the 25-day period into five contiguous segments of equal length. The segments represent constant, periodic, piecewise-constant, ramp, and random-walk inputs, and their order varies between profiles. Across all segment types, injection rates remain between 0 and #mg-min[500000], and the change between two consecutive 30-minute values does not exceed #mg-min[50000].

These profiles are used exclusively in the precomputed offline dataset, and their complete generation procedure is documented in @section:appendix:chlorine-profile-generation.


=== Fixed Arsenic Schedule Sets

A finite set of arsenic schedules is generated for the precomputed offline trajectories using the event-generation procedure described in @section:dataset:arsenic-events. Five schedules are generated for each hydraulic base scenario.


=== Trajectory Construction and Stored Variables

The complete pairing of five prescribed chlorine profiles and five fixed arsenic schedules makes a total of 25 trajectories per scenario and 2500 trajectories across the 100 hydraulic base scenarios. Every complete trajectory contains 1201 reported samples, including the initial state and the final state after 25 days, generated by the coupled EPANET and #acr("EPANET-MSX") simulation.

Each trajectory contains the time series of the network variables described in @section:dataset:simulation-setup, together with the applied chlorine injection profile and the corresponding arsenic schedule. The selection of model inputs and prediction targets is described in @section:methodology:forecasting-data.

@fig:representative-simulation-run illustrates the chlorine and arsenic injections and the resulting nodal concentrations over a complete 25-day trajectory.

